1844738</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="ref11"><label>11</label><citation citation-type="journal">Boomsma DI, van Beijsterveldt CE, Rietveld MJ, Bartels M, van Baal GC. Genetics mediate relation of birth weight to childhood IQ. <source>BMJ</source><year>2001</year>;<volume>323</volume>:<fpage>1426</fpage>-7.</citation></ref><ref id="ref12"><label>12</label><citation citation-type="journal">Jefferis BJ, Power C, Hertzman C. Birth weight, childhood socioeconomic environment, and cognitive development in the 1958 British birth cohort study. <source>BMJ</source><year>2002</year>;<volume>325</volume>:<fpage>305</fpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12169505</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="ref13"><label>13</label><citation citation-type="journal">Shenkin SD, Starr JM, Patti A, Rush MA, Whalley LJ, Deary IJ. Birth weight and cognitive function at age 11 years: the Scottish mental survey 1932. <source>Arch Dis Child</source><year>2001</year>;<volume>85</volume>:<fpage>189</fpage>-97.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11517097</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="ref14"><label>14</label><citation citation-type="journal">Lin MJ, Liu JT, Chou SY. As low birth weight babies grow, can well-educated parents buffer this adverse factor? A research note.<source> Demography</source><year>2007</year>;<volume>44</volume>:<fpage>335</fpage>-43.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17583308</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="ref15"><label>15</label><citation citation-type="journal">Black SE, Devereux PJ, Salvanes KG. The more the merrier? The effect of family size and birth order on children&#x02019;s education. <source>Q J Econ</source><year>2005</year>;<volume>120</volume>:<fpage>669</fpage>-700.</citation></ref><ref id="ref16"><label>16</label><citation citation-type="other">Conley D, Strully K, Bennett NG. <source>A pound of flesh or just proxy? Using twin differences to estimate the effect of birth weight on life chances</source> Cambridge, MA:  National Bureau of Economic Research, <year>2003</year> (working paper No 9901).</citation></ref><ref id="ref17"><label>17</label><citation citation-type="journal">Black SE, Devereux PJ, Salvanes KG. From the cradle to the labor market? The effect of birth weight on adult outcomes. <source>Q J Econ</source><year>2007</year>;<volume>122</volume>:<fpage>409</fpage>-39.</citation></ref><ref id="ref18"><label>18</label><citation citation-type="journal">Regan TL, Oaxaca RL, Burghardt G. A human capital model of the effects of ability and family background on optimal schooling levels. <source>Econ Inq</source><year>2007</year>;<volume>45</volume>:<fpage>721</fpage>-38.</citation></ref><ref id="ref19"><label>19</label><citation citation-type="journal">Behrman JR, Rosenzweig MR. Returns to birthweight. <source>Rev Econ Stat</source><year>2004</year>;<volume>86</volume>:<fpage>586</fpage>-601.</citation></ref><ref id="ref20"><label>20</label><citation citation-type="journal">Olsen J. The association between birth weight, placenta weight, pregnancy duration, subfecundity, and child development. <source>Scand J Soc Med</source><year>1994</year>;<volume>22</volume>:<fpage>213</fpage>-8.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">7531363</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="ref21"><label>21</label><citation citation-type="journal">S&#x000f8;rensen HT, Sabroe S, Olsen J, Rothman KJ, Gillman MW, Fischer P. Birth weight and cognitive function in young adult life: historical cohort study. <source>BMJ</source><year>1997</year>;<volume>315</volume>:<fpage>401</fpage>-3.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9277604</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="ref22"><label>22</label><citation citation-type="journal">Lawlor DA, Clark H, Smith GD, Leon DA. Intrauterine growth and intelligence within sibling pairs: findings from the Aberdeen children of the 1950s cohort. <source>Pediatrics</source><year>2006</year>;<volume>117</volume>:<fpage>e894</fpage>-902.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16651293</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="ref23"><label>23</label><citation citation-type="journal">Maureen HM, Flannery DJ, Schluchter M, Carter L, Borawski E, Klein N. Outcomes in young adulthood for very-low-birth-weight infants. <source>N Engl J Med</source><year>2002</year>;<volume>346</volume>:<fpage>149</fpage>-57.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11796848</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="ref24"><label>24</label><citation citation-type="journal">Evans DM, Martin NG. The validity of twin studies. <source>GeneScreen</source><year>2000</year>;<volume>1</volume>:<fpage>77</fpage>-9.</citation></ref></ref-list><notes notes-type="citation-text"><p><bold>Cite this as:</bold> <italic>BMJ</italic> 2008;337:a438</p></notes></back></article>